                "text": "We need teach by showing pairs. Need understand rule: split left/right halves, output 4 where both cells black (0), else 0. Input 4x8 with colors 0,6,5 (magenta=6? grey=5). Output 4x4 with 4 yellow and 0 black.\n\nWe need provide demonstration inputs. The verifier computes output. We can choose arbitrary 4x8 grids using colors 0,6,5. Need perhaps diverse examples to infer rule. We want student to infer: compare left and right halves, output 4 where both zero. Show several inputs covering cases.\n\nWe can call show_transformed_input multiple times. Each input must be 4x8 rectangular integers 0-9. Let's create varied patterns with some zeros/nonzeros. Ensure not degenerate too much. We can include cases where left zero right nonzero -> 0; left nonzero right zero->0; both nonzero ->0; both zero->4. Also maybe all zeros / all filled.\n\nLet's design 3-5 pairs.\n\nExample 1: all zeros? Then output all 4. Shows both halves black. But maybe too trivial.\n\nExample 2: identity left all 6, right all 5? Output all 0 because no both zero. Hmm.\n\nBetter create mixed.\n\nLet's define rows as concatenation L|R.\n\nPair 1:\nrow0 L [0,6,0,6] R [0,5,0,5]\nrow1 L [6,0,6,0] R [5,0,5,0]\nrow2 L [0,0,6,6] R [0,0,5,5]\nrow3 L [6,6,0,0] R [5,5,0,0]\n\nOutput where both zero:\nrow0 col0 L0 R0 ->4; c1 6,5 no; c2 0,0 ->4; c3 6,5 no => [4,0,4,0]\nrow1 c0 6,5 no; c1 0,0 ->4; c2 6,5 no; c3 0,0 ->4 => [0,4,0,4]\nrow2 c0 0,0 4; c1 0,0 4; c2 6,5 no; c3 6,5 no => [4,4,0,0]\nrow3 [0,0,4,4]. Good.\n\nPair 2: varied where both non-zero but maybe same? colors 6 and 5, sum nonzero anyway. output 0. Need some both zero.\n\nInput:\nrow0 L [6,6,0,0] R [5,0,5,0]\nrow1 L [0,6,0,6] R [0,5,5,0]\nrow2 L [6,0,6,0] R [5,0,0,5]\nrow3 L [0,0,0,0] R [5,5,5,5]\n\nOutput:\nrow0: c0 6,5 no; c1 6,0 no (L nonzero); c2 0,5 no; c3 0,0 ->4 => [0,0,0,4]\nrow1: c0 0,0 ->4; c1 6,5 no; c2 0,5 no; c3 6,0 no => [4,0,0,0]\nrow2: c0 6,5 no; c1 0,0 ->4; c2 6,0 no; c3 0,5 no => [0,4,0,0]\nrow3: c0 0,5 no; c1 0,5 no; c2 0,5 no; c3 0,5 no => all 0. Good.\n\nPair 3: maybe left/right swapped patterns. Input:\nrow0 L [0,0,0,0] R [5,5,5,5]\nrow1 L [6,6,6,6] R [0,0,0,0]\nrow2 L [0,6,0,6] R [5,0,5,0]\nrow3 L [6,0,6,0] R [0,5,0,5]\n\nOutput:\nrow0 L zeros R nonzero => all 0\nrow1 L nonzero R zeros => all 0\nrow2 c0 0,5 no; c1 6,0 no; c2 0,5 no; c3 6,0 no => all 0\nrow3 c0 6,0 no; c1 0,5 no; c2 6,0 no; c3 0,5 no => all 0. Hmm all zeros output, not informative maybe but boundary.\n\nPair 4: all zeros input => output all 4. Shows both zero condition.\n\nPair 5: all nonzero both sides => all 0.\n\nMaybe 4 examples sufficient. The student sees only these pairs. Need infer output colors: yellow 4 and black 0. We must show input grids use 0,5,6. Output computed.\n\nLet's ensure verifier uses np.where(added==0,4,0), so input can have any colors; if left+right=0 only when both zero (nonnegative). Good.\n\nWe can show 4-6 pairs. More examples can help but not too many. Let's include one with input where halves identical zeros positions. Need show mapping positions: output 4 at positions where both zero. To make robust, vary shapes.\n\nLet's create 5 pairs.\n\nPair 1 as above.\nPair 2 as above.\nPair 3 with complementary zeros pattern perhaps:\nInput:\nrow0 L [0,6,0,6] R [5,5,5,5] -> c0 0+5=5, c1 6+5, c2 0+5, c3 6+5 => all 0\nrow1 L [6,0,6,0] R [5,5,5,5] -> all 0\nrow2 L [0,6,0,6] R [0,5,0,5] -> c0 0+0=0 ->4; c1 6+5; c2 0+0 ->4; c3 6+5 => [4,0,4,0]\nrow3 L [6,0,6,0] R [5,0,5,0] -> c0 6+5; c1 0+0 ->4; c2 6+5; c3 0+0 ->4 => [0,4,0,4]\nOutput:\n[0,0,0,0]\n[0,0,0,0]\n[4,0,4,0]\n[0,4,0,4]\nThis is like pair1 but top rows differ.\n\nPair 4 all zeros: input 4x8 all 0 -> output all 4. This may be useful.\n\nPair 5 maybe random:\nrow0 L [0,6,0,0] R [0,5,5,0]\nrow1 L [6,0,6,0] R [5,5,0,0]\nrow2 L [0,0,0,6] R [5,0,0,5]\nrow3 L [6,6,0,0] R [0,5,0,5]\nCompute:\nrow0: c0 0+0=0 ->4; c1 6+5=11 ->0; c2 0+5 ->0; c3 0+0 ->4 => [4,0,0,4]\nrow1: c0 6+5; c1 0+5; c2 6+0; c3 0+0 ->4 => [0,0,0,4]\nrow2: c0 0+5; c1 0+0 ->4; c2 0+0 ->4; c3 6+5 => [0,4,4,0]\nrow3: c0 6+0; c1 6+5; c2 0+0 ->4; c3 0+5 => [0,0,4,0]\nGood.\n\nMaybe another with some both nonzero but zero sum impossible. Already fine.\n\nWe need call show_transformed_input for each. Then finish_teaching. The prompt says call start_exam? Actually tools: finish_teaching. We should call show_transformed_input with input_grid. No prose needed maybe. We'll do multiple calls.\n\nNeed ensure JSON input format list of lists. Let's proceed.\n\nWe might consider that student sees pairs but not rule. We should make demonstration set clear. Maybe outputs 4 yellow. Input colors 6 (magenta) and 5 (grey). Good.\n\nLet's submit.",
